// @emit-error an i32-keyed Map/Set is supported as a binding / parameter / return / `| null` / an ARRAY ELEMENT / a closure result / a map value
// CAPABILITY (B6b): the array-element position OPENED, and what is left of it.
//
// The supported set is now a binding, a parameter, a return, `| null`, an ARRAY ELEMENT
// (`{[i32]: V}[]`, and the `({[i32]: V} | null)[]` niche), a closure RESULT and a MAP
// VALUE, with every VALUE type the string-keyed rep lowers. The struct/variant FIELD is
// the last position an i32-keyed map cannot occupy.
//
// #1286 kept the array element rejecting DELIBERATELY, because the `{[string]: V}`
// spelling of a list of maps was itself invalid wasm for 12 of 15 value types and
// opening the i32 half would have converted a clean reject into that. That is fixed
// (`list-of-maps-element-values.vl`), so the position opened — for one `[]` peel in
// `i32MapSpellingLowerable`, exactly as that filing predicted: a ref-list ELEMENT row
// records the whole `{[i32]: V}` spelling, unlike a field row, and needed no new column.
// `list-of-maps-i32-keyed.vl` runs the whole surface.
//
// WHAT STILL REJECTS IS ONE RUNG IN, and the peel is deliberately ONE `[]` deep. A list
// of LISTS of maps puts the map behind a SECOND ref-list element row — the inner list's,
// reached through `rlElemInnerSlot` — and nothing has yet asked whether that row carries
// the key the way a direct element row does. The string-keyed twin of this program runs,
// so the reject is a position gate and not a value gate; opening it is a measurement,
// not a guess, which is the same discipline the closure-ARRAY note in that function
// states for `(() => {[i32]: i32})[]`.
const xs: {[i32]: i32}[][] = [[Map()]]
xs[0][0][1] = 2
print(xs[0][0].length)
